  "anomalies_ranked": [
   {
    "rank": 1,
    "id": "ORPHAN-BRIEFS",
    "finding": "136 task briefs on disk (and 31 committed findings files) have no kanban row in store+archive \u2014 the delegation-evidence record is blind to ~19% of documented work; the T6xx orphans are recent (2026-08-20+), not archaeology.",
    "query": "Q-ORPHAN / Q-ORPHAN-FINDINGS (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 2,
    "id": "STALE-DELIVERABLES",
    "finding": "53 pass rows cite a deliverable absent from disk today; 37 paths are one tree move (docs/epic-01-markovian -> docs/epics/E1-markovian), 20 are per-task relocations (bin/ollama-subagent -> bin/subagent, docs/infra/races/* -> S02-model-delegation, tools/claimlint.py -> src/claimlint.zig, assertion-ledger retired). The T278 close-time check (asks git at close) cannot catch post-close moves; deliverables= is load-bearing for scope, so stale paths are a scope-measurement hazard.",
    "query": "Q-ABSENT (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 3,
    "id": "FAIL-FOUND-CONCENTRATED",
    "finding": "4 fail-found closes in 578 rows, 3 of them audit-type, all on battery/audit work; no fail-found ever recorded for infra/implement/spec/research/integration/orchestration. Pass+pfw rate is 91-100% for every model with >=3 closes.",
    "query": "Q-FAIL / Q-PASS-MODEL (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 4,
    "id": "KILL-CENSUS-CONFOUNDED",
    "finding": "4,196 'exit 124' lines in 90 task-logs, dominated by kill-instrumentation tasks (T625 1,112; T643; T773; T629) whose subject IS the kill path; the runner's outer run records show exit 0 for the same tasks. The raw log census (model-profiles' efficiency signal) measures kill-path testing, not task efficiency.",
    "query": "Q-KILL / Q-RUNS-EXIT (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 5,
    "id": "ACCEPTANCE-SILENT-AT-CLOSE",
    "finding": "73 of 539 briefs-on-disk rows (13.5%) mention acceptance/gate in close text; whether acceptance conditions were checked at close is not recorded for the other ~87%.",
    "query": "Q-ACC-CLOSE (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 6,
    "id": "UNKNOWN-IS-REAL",
    "finding": "20 rows (3.5%) have no classifiable type signal (TL1* dummies, STANDING-CLEANUP, T762/T763 no-brief, ten thin-brief rows); never fold them into a bucket.",
    "query": "classify.py --main"
   },
   {
    "rank": 7,
    "id": "RETRY-RHYTHM",
    "finding": "89 rows claimed >1 time; flat across models (flash 24/pro 22/orcha 12), concentrated in audit (33)/infra (20)/implement (15); claim hours peak at 12:00Z and 22:00Z \u2014 a schedule rhythm, not a uniform process.",
    "query": "Q-RETRY (anomalies.py)"
   },
   {
    "rank": 8,
    "id": "SCOPE-ORDINAL-HOLDS",
    "finding": "the scope enumeration is monotone in measured wall and RSS: S1 (337 s median, 362 MB) < S2 (1,276 s, 524 MB) < S3 (2,325 s, 851 MB); S4 (647 s) sits between S1 and S2 as 'bounded read-only'; S5 (1,017 s) between S2 and S3.",
    "query": "Q-SCOPE-ORDINAL (anomalies.py)"
   }
  ]
 },
 "notes": "T817 mined task type, scope and capabilities retroactively for the delegation-evidence lattice (L1). 578 corpus rows in docs/infra/task-corpus.jsonl; method, classifier accuracy, scope defence, capability vocabulary and limits in docs/infra/task-corpus.md. Every derived value carries basis+confidence; UNKNOWN is a value, never a fill. Scope enumeration adopted from the S07 lattice spec (LAT-DIM-5) with two documented data-driven amendments; capabilities derived from corpus demand-phrases with hand-checked example tasks. Calibration: brief-aware classifier 100% vs 78% legacy on the 27-row self-declared+race holdout; Landmark line shown to be a domain signal, not a type signal (negative result). No live schema file touched (tasks.json/archive.json read-only via snapshot; src/managent/main.zig untouched; model-task-metrics.jsonl untouched). Repro scripts committed under docs/evidence/T817-task-corpus/ (lib.py, classify.py, mine.py, calib.py, anomalies.py); T817_SNAPSHOT_DIR reproduces the committed corpus byte-for-byte."
}